# Aliases in an interactive login bash on Ubuntu 22.04. Definition, the listing format (sorted by
# name, `alias NAME='VALUE'`, `--` before a name that starts with `-`), the refusals (a name with a
# blank, a quote, `$` or `/` is `invalid alias name`; `-x` is `invalid option` with a usage line),
# expansion at command position (after `;`, `&&`, `|`, `(`, `then`, `do`, an assignment and a
# redirection, never in a pattern or after a quote), the trailing blank that lets the next word be
# an alias, a value that holds an operator, a quote or a here-document, a name that is not expanded
# inside its own value, and `type` and `command -v`. An alias takes effect from the next line, so
# the lines that define and use one are separate. Each reply was produced by
# `bash --noprofile --norc --noediting -i` started as a login shell (`exec -a -bash`) in the
